WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:03.190
Sinewy chrome legs...

00:00:03.190 --> 00:00:05.120
Elegant caned seat...

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:08.315
Rich birch wood...

00:00:08.315 --> 00:00:10.625
Introducing, the cesca....

00:00:12.254 --> 00:00:14.074
What's like, the right amount of creepy for
this?

00:00:16.033 --> 00:00:19.152
The cesca, also know as the b32

00:00:19.152 --> 00:00:21.189
b64 if it has arms

00:00:21.189 --> 00:00:24.000
is kind of having a moment.

00:00:25.222 --> 00:00:29.079
Full disclosure, I am literally sitting on
one right now.

00:00:29.079 --> 00:00:31.958
Have been since at least 1999.

00:00:31.958 --> 00:00:36.743
But this chair is more than just a throne
for toddler-me to practice my penmanship.

00:00:36.743 --> 00:00:38.881
It’s more than a trendy decor item.

00:00:38.881 --> 00:00:40.781
Or popular movie set piece.

00:00:40.781 --> 00:00:42.857
It’s a design icon.

00:00:42.857 --> 00:00:46.092
In the collections of some of the world’s
most major museums.

00:00:46.092 --> 00:00:50.033
And considered “among the most important
chairs of the 20th century.”

00:00:50.033 --> 00:00:52.484
So where did this chair come from?

00:00:52.484 --> 00:00:54.000
And why is it everywhere?

00:01:01.065 --> 00:01:03.730
The cesca chairs story begins here.

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:05.183
At the Bauhaus.

00:01:05.183 --> 00:01:11.214
The famed German art school slash commune
slash never-ending party slash genius factory.

00:01:11.214 --> 00:01:15.000
It was founded in 1919 by this architect,
Walter Gropius.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:17.754
His goal was to merge art and industry.

00:01:17.754 --> 00:01:23.860
Creating work that was deeply modern and simultaneously
beautiful, functional, and reproducible.

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:25.799
Which was a pretty radical change from

00:01:25.799 --> 00:01:30.133
the exclusivity and ornamental frills of other
design movements.

00:01:30.133 --> 00:01:33.392
Look at these two teapots both from
1920s Germany.

00:01:33.392 --> 00:01:35.933
This one is beautiful, but mostly decorative.

00:01:35.933 --> 00:01:38.733
On the Bauhaus one, everything is intentional.

00:01:38.733 --> 00:01:41.319
The curved, wooden handle makes pouring easy
and comfortable.

00:01:41.319 --> 00:01:43.159
The slanted spout prevents drips.

00:01:43.159 --> 00:01:46.249
And it definitely looks sturdy and industrial.

00:01:46.249 --> 00:01:51.209
Except this teapot was hand crafted out of
silver and ebony.

00:01:51.209 --> 00:01:54.000
It was wildly expensive.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:56.300
Still is.

00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:59.691
Even though Bauhaus designs looked utilitarian.

00:01:59.691 --> 00:02:03.685
Most of them were basically impossible 
to make at scale.

00:02:03.685 --> 00:02:07.210
That is, until our hero, Marcel Breuer

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:09.300
Mr. Cesca chair himself

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:11.763
stepped in and changed everything.

00:02:13.129 --> 00:02:15.610
Breuer was an early student of the Bauhaus

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:22.264
and in 1925, he was ruminating on their whole
manufacturing problem when inspiration struck.

00:02:22.264 --> 00:02:25.560
He looked at his bicycle’s handlebars and
realized

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:29.765
to paraphrase: Bent tubular steel
was Bauhaus as heck.

00:02:29.765 --> 00:02:34.569
It’s sleek, light, shiny, strong and —
this one’s a direct quote

00:02:34.569 --> 00:02:36.994
can be bent “like macaroni”.

00:02:36.994 --> 00:02:38.160
First, he made this chair.

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:40.359
Now known as the Wassily chair.

00:02:40.359 --> 00:02:43.170
And then a bunch of other chairs and tables
and stools

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:45.069
and this... couch?

00:02:45.563 --> 00:02:48.844
But, for Breuer, those pieces still weren’t
modern enough.

00:02:48.844 --> 00:02:51.256
The ultimate creation, he wrote, 
would be a

00:02:51.256 --> 00:02:55.006
“chair that floated on an elastic column
of air.”

00:02:55.006 --> 00:02:59.060
And in 1928 Breuer had another one of those
eureka moments.

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:03.959
He flipped a stool on its side and thus
the cantilever chair was born.

00:03:03.959 --> 00:03:07.135
Soon after, he debuted the b32.

00:03:07.135 --> 00:03:12.129
And with it, he achieved the purest manifestation
of Bauhaus ideals.

00:03:12.129 --> 00:03:15.154
A chair that showcased the gleaming modernity
of chrome.

00:03:15.154 --> 00:03:17.440
Seemed to defy the laws of gravity.

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:22.692
And, crucially, only required a handful of
pre-made materials to make.

00:03:22.692 --> 00:03:27.921
Soon, Thonet — a company already world famous
for mass producing bent wooden chairs

00:03:27.921 --> 00:03:30.489
was making tons of b32s.

00:03:30.489 --> 00:03:33.330
And as other Bauhausians designed their own
versions

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:35.920
a bunch of other tubular cantilever chairs.

00:03:36.562 --> 00:03:40.660
But it’s pretty universally held that this
is the best of them.

00:03:40.660 --> 00:03:43.325
Most cantilever chairs require braces.

00:03:43.325 --> 00:03:48.480
Which both ruin the visual lines of the chair
and makes them rigid and uncomfortable.

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:52.192
Breuer’s chair doesn’t need those, thanks to 
its structured wooden framing

00:03:52.192 --> 00:03:56.922
which holds everything together, but 
still allows for flexibility and bounce.

00:03:56.922 --> 00:04:01.819
That added structure also means the chair
can be made of one continuous length of steel

00:04:01.819 --> 00:04:06.439
which is bent 16 times rather than a bunch
of different tubes fused together.

00:04:06.439 --> 00:04:09.340
which makes the chair lighter and easier to
make.

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:13.261
Plus the cane gave the chair an airy transparency.

00:04:13.261 --> 00:04:16.472
A feature Thonet played up, in their 1930s advertising.

00:04:16.472 --> 00:04:18.834
Of which there was a lot.

00:04:18.834 --> 00:04:21.594
Thonet really wanted these designs to take off.

00:04:21.594 --> 00:04:24.102
The problem was they were expensive.

00:04:24.102 --> 00:04:27.452
And they seemed a little too modern for the
average home.

00:04:27.452 --> 00:04:31.466
Until the 60s.

00:04:31.466 --> 00:04:35.250
A sort of hybrid futuristic look became all the rage

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:38.320
and the b32 fit in perfectly.

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:42.120
Gavina, the go-to Italian modernist brand,
began selling it.

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:44.410
And they gave it a new name: Cesca

00:04:44.410 --> 00:04:47.453
from Francesca, Breuer’s daughter’s name.

00:04:47.453 --> 00:04:51.000
As the mid-century look picked up steam,
so did the cesca.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:56.613
But unlike those newer designs the cesca wasnt
copyrighted.

00:04:56.613 --> 00:05:01.423
So manufacturers started making completely
legal cesca copies.

00:05:01.423 --> 00:05:04.685
And marketing them as “Breuer style chairs”.

00:05:07.491 --> 00:05:10.719
By 1980, the cesca was ubiquitous.

00:05:10.719 --> 00:05:13.513
One reviewer noted it was “as common, as
imitated...

00:05:13.513 --> 00:05:16.870
and as mass produced as a 
'pair of Calvin Klein jeans'"

00:05:16.870 --> 00:05:19.930
Which, at the time, was just about the highest
praise there was.

00:05:19.930 --> 00:05:22.830
After that, the cesca never quite went away.

00:05:22.830 --> 00:05:25.195
Today, there are many places to buy one

00:05:25.195 --> 00:05:28.245
and countless second hand ones floating around.

00:05:28.245 --> 00:05:30.903
Which only seems to drive up demand.

00:05:30.903 --> 00:05:36.551
Its appeal is only getting broader as both
Bauhaus and modernism become popular again.

00:05:36.551 --> 00:05:39.430
In short, this chair is everywhere because

00:05:39.430 --> 00:05:42.692
ever since its inception nearly a century
ago,

00:05:42.692 --> 00:05:44.830
it's been a design marvel.

00:05:44.830 --> 00:05:47.266
And, quite frankly, cool as hell.

